# Vampire Survivors + Jev project

The owner is an experienced data engineer who is new to C# game modding, Unity, and IL2CPP. Explain unfamiliar concepts before using them and prefer small, verifiable changes.

The current milestone is v1 polar pilot:

- load through BepInEx 6 IL2CPP (`winhttp.dll`); keep MelonLoader's `version.dll` disabled — it crashes this Unity 6 install in coreclr.dll;
- `UnityLogListening = false`; never retry Unity `AddComponent`;
- Harmony postfix on existing `GameManager.Update` publishes game-state v4 over UDP 127.0.0.1 (source `bepinex`): 3×8 polar occupancy for enemies, XP and walls (`PhaserTilemap.IsTileAtPosition`), inner-ring enemies with velocity, bosses, chests, player level/XP/magnet/facing/weapons and Phaser body blocked sides; v4 is additive over v3 and every new field is optional;
- every new game read is isolated in its own try/catch, logs once to `debug-*.log`, and disables itself for the session; heavier probes have `Telemetry.ProbeWalls` / `Telemetry.ProbeWeapons` config switches;
- compact overlay HUD shows Hello world, player x,y/HP, level and XP %, weapons, hottest XP sector, inner enemy count, seq Hz, paused/level-up, and the pilot's mode and blocked sides;
- on-game field overlay is a WinForms layer (not Unity AddComponent): player at the center, rings scaled to the camera view, wall cells in grey, blocked sides in red;
- the Python pilot (`bridge/vampirejev/pilot.py` + `policy.py`) owns memory (displacement stuck detector, wall memory, body-flag calibration, mode/heading hysteresis) and the 8-way scoring (priced contact, phase dial, openness, kiting, wall costs, weapon aim classes, farm/kite/flee/chest modes), WASD SendInput, and arm/focus/pause gates; tap Ctrl to arm; `VSJEV_NUDGE` and `--mode` still override;
- weapon knowledge stops at aim class (facing / movement / auto), keyed by `WeaponType` enum names; no DPS estimation, no destructibles, no Jev in this pass;
- policy changes need a `sim.py` scenario or a `test_policy.py` case; `python -m vampirejev.replay` re-runs JSONL recordings offline;
- do not call Jev from the in-game DLL;
- do not add API keys or network calls beyond localhost UDP.

Do not guess Vampire Survivors internal classes or fields. First inspect the generated assemblies from the user's installation and record evidence. Keep game-specific adapters separate from normalized telemetry contracts. Put future Jev calls in the external bridge, where credentials and policy can be managed safely.
